(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-misc-bacterial-agents-quiz"]={"title":"Miscellaneous Bacterial Agents — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does Vibrio cholerae infect the small intestine?","choices":["By raising its pH with urease","By entering endothelial cells","Without invading its lining","By invading the intestinal wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Vibrio cholerae infects the mucous barrier of the small intestine and is noninvasive; its toxin causes the secretory diarrhea. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which infections can Campylobacter fetus cause in a newborn?","choices":["Hutchinson's teeth","Meningitis and septicemia","Inclusion conjunctivitis","Snuffles and bone deformity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Campylobacter fetus can cause meningitis, pneumonia, arthritis and septicemia in the newborn. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How does a fetus acquire syphilis?","choices":["From the mother's urine","Across the placenta","Through the birth canal","From a tick bite in infancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: congenital syphilis is a trans-placental infection of the fetus, causing nasal discharge, skin eruptions, bone deformation and nervous system abnormalities after birth. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 6 and 10"},{"q":"How do Borrelia cells differ from the other spirochetes in their coils?","choices":["Thin, with regular coils","Tight coils with hooked ends","No coils, a comma shape","Large, with irregular coils"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Borrelia cells are large, with 3-10 irregularly spaced coils, unlike the thin regular coils of Treponema or the tight hooked coils of Leptospira. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 5, 21 and 23"},{"q":"In cholera, which way do water and electrolytes move across the intestinal cell?","choices":["Out of the cell into the lumen","From the lumen into the blood","Into the cell, which then swells","Out of the gut into the lymph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: normally water and electrolytes are absorbed from the lumen into the blood; cholera toxin reverses this, so water and electrolytes (chloride and potassium among them) are lost into the lumen. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 32 and image on Slide 33"},{"q":"Which stage of syphilis begins with a hard chancre at the site of inoculation?","choices":["Congenital","Primary","Secondary","Tertiary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: primary syphilis is the appearance of a hard chancre where the organism entered; the chancre often heals on its own. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which description fits Campylobacter?","choices":["Comma-shaped single rods","Tight coils with hooked ends","Wall-less, highly variable cells","Curved rods in gull-winged pairs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: campylobacters are Gram-negative slender, curved or spiral rods, often S-shaped or gull-winged pairs, with a single polar flagellum. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 36, 21 and 62"},{"q":"For discovering which organism did Warren and Marshall receive the Nobel Prize in Medicine?","choices":["Vibrio cholerae","Coxiella burnetii","Helicobacter pylori","Treponema pallidum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Warren and Marshall, Australian doctors, discovered Helicobacter pylori in stomach biopsies from ulcer patients and received the Nobel Prize in Medicine in 2005. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why is tetracycline a treatment for so many different bacteria, from Borrelia to Mycoplasma?","choices":["Broad spectrum, 30S ribosome target","Peptidoglycan synthesis blockade","Selective Gram-negative wall attack","Replacement of lost electrolytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: tetracycline is a very broad-spectrum antibiotic that targets prokaryotic ribosomes, at the 30S subunit; it is named for relapsing fever, Lyme disease, cholera, Q fever, Bartonella and Mycoplasma. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 53, 24, 26, 32, 52 and 62"},{"q":"Which vibrio causes gastroenteritis from raw seafood with symptoms similar to cholera?","choices":["Vibrio vulnificus","Vibrio cholerae El Tor","Campylobacter fetus","Vibrio parahaemolyticus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Vibrio parahaemolyticus causes gastroenteritis from raw seafood, with symptoms similar to cholera. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How is trench fever spread?","choices":["By tick bites","By inhaled dust","By lice","By cat scratches"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: trench fever, caused by Bartonella, is spread by lice. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How does Helicobacter differ from Vibrio and Campylobacter in its flagella?","choices":["It has several polar flagella","It has a single polar flagellum","It has no flagella","It uses pili, not flagella"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Vibrio and Campylobacter each have a single polar flagellum; Helicobacter has several polar flagella. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 31 and 39"},{"q":"Which chlamydial form is the infectious one released by the infected host cell?","choices":["The reticulate body","The endospore","The inclusion body","The elementary body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the elementary body is small, metabolically inactive, extracellular and infectious, released by the infected host cell; the reticulate body is the noninfectious dividing form. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 56 and image on Slide 57"},{"q":"Which Leptospira species is a harmless, free-living saprobe?","choices":["Treponema carateum","Leptospira biflexa","Leptospira interrogans","Borrelia hermsii"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Leptospira biflexa is a harmless free-living saprobe, while Leptospira interrogans causes leptospirosis. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is penicillin not used against Mycoplasma?","choices":["They form resistant endospores","Their outer membrane blocks it","They have no peptidoglycan","They live inside host cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: mycoplasmas naturally lack cell walls, so no peptidoglycan is present; they are treated with tetracycline or erythromycin instead. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 42 and 62"},{"q":"How does the transmission of the nonsyphilitic treponematoses differ from that of syphilis?","choices":["Mostly by inhalation","Mostly by nonsexual routes","Mostly by tick bites","Mostly through animal urine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the nonsyphilitic (non-venereal) treponematoses resemble syphilis but are rarely transmitted sexually or congenitally; they are cutaneous and bone diseases endemic to particular regions. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 19, 22 and 52"},{"q":"Why might erythromycin or tetracycline be used to treat syphilis?","choices":["The disease is tertiary","The patient is pregnant","Penicillin is contraindicated","They work better than penicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: erythromycin and tetracycline are less effective than penicillin G or doxycycline but can be used when penicillin is contraindicated, for example by patient allergy. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In the Lyme disease tick cycle, on which animal do nymphs mature into adult ticks that mate?","choices":["White-footed mice","Humans","Wild birds","Deer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in the second year nymphs feed on several hosts, including deer and humans; on deer they mature into adult male and female ticks, which mate, and the female lays eggs in plant litter. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 26 and image on Slide 28"},{"q":"Which vector carries Rickettsia prowazekii, the cause of epidemic typhus?","choices":["Body lice","Fleas","Ticks","Mites"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: epidemic typhus is caused by Rickettsia prowazekii, carried by lice; louse feces are rubbed into the bite, and humans are the primary reservoir. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 45 and image on Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the bull's-eye rash at the site of the tick bite in Lyme disease called?","choices":["Erythema migrans","A gumma","A hard chancre","Bacillary angiomatosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: erythema migrans is the bull's-eye rash at the initial tick-bite location in Lyme disease. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 26 and 30"},{"q":"Which description fits Rickettsia?","choices":["Large spirals with endoflagella","Wall-less, highly variable cells","Comma rods with one flagellum","Tiny, nonmotile Gram-negative rods"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: rickettsias are obligate intracellular parasites with a Gram-negative wall, among the smallest bacteria, nonmotile pleomorphic rods or coccobacilli. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 42 and 44"},{"q":"Because it mimics neuromuscular and rheumatoid conditions, Lyme disease can be mistaken for what?","choices":["An autoimmune disorder","A secretory diarrhea","An atypical pneumonia","A peptic ulcer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Lyme disease is a nonfatal, slowly progressive syndrome that mimics neuromuscular and rheumatoid conditions and could be mistaken for an autoimmune disorder. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which lesions develop in tertiary syphilis?","choices":["Hard chancres","Palm and sole rashes","Hutchinson's teeth","Gummas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: tertiary syphilis can last 20 years or longer; neurological and cardiovascular symptoms develop and gummas form in organs. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 10 and 14"},{"q":"How does dental plaque damage tooth enamel?","choices":["It blocks the tooth's blood","Its bacteria make acid","Its bacteria make urease","It hardens into the gums"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: plaque harbors masses of bacteria that produce acid, which dissolves the enamel, leading to dental caries. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the best treatment option for seafood vibrio gastroenteritis?","choices":["Tetracycline or amoxicillin","Rifampin or erythromycin","Fluid and electrolyte replacement","Penicillin G or doxycycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: for the seafood vibrios, fluid and electrolyte replacement is the best option; antimicrobial drugs are used only occasionally. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which chlamydial disease deforms the eyelid and cornea and may cause blindness?","choices":["Ocular trachoma","Inclusion conjunctivitis","Lymphogranuloma venereum","Ornithosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: ocular trachoma is a severe infection that deforms the eyelid and cornea and may cause blindness. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is periodontitis?","choices":["Acid damage to the enamel","Mineralized plaque on teeth","Infection reaching the tooth socket","Gum inflammation from calculus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: when bacteria invade the pockets between tooth and gingiva, the tooth socket may become involved (periodontitis) and the tooth may be lost. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"In a spirochete, where do the endoflagella lie?","choices":["In the periplasmic space","Outside the outer membrane","Within a thick capsule","Inside the cytoplasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the endoflagella run in the periplasmic space, under the outer membrane and along the cell body, which is why they are called endoflagella. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 3 and image on Slide 4"},{"q":"Which vectors carry Rocky Mountain spotted fever?","choices":["Rat fleas","Dog and wood ticks","Ixodes ticks","Body lice"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Rocky Mountain spotted fever is a Rickettsia rickettsii zoonosis carried by dog and wood ticks; the cycle runs between ticks and dogs, with humans infected by tick bite. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 46 and image on Slide 49"},{"q":"A false-positive rapid plasma reagin result for syphilis can occur in a patient with which condition?","choices":["Trench fever","Lyme disease","Cholera","Dental caries"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: syphilis tests can give false positives with human immunodeficiency virus infection, Lyme disease, some pneumonias, malaria and lupus. — PAJ5200.Misc inf agents-2.pptx, Slide 16"}]};
